  knees up on a cushion and the radio
  on, and she did not know yet what the
  job was. Walt had not been able to
  make the words come out at breakfast.

  The new ferry was steel and fast and
  ran on batteries. It was a fine boat.
  Everyone said so. The [3mBluebell[0m had sat
  at Pier 9 for two years with a tarp
  over her wheelhouse while the town
  council argued about museums and
  grants and insurance, and in the end
  the arguing had simply stopped, the
  way arguing does when everybody gets
  tired at the same time.

  His deckhand, a lanky
  nineteen-year-old named Teo, had the
  lines ready. "Straight shot down the
  channel?" he asked. "We could be at
  Harrow by eleven."

  Walt looked at the [3mBluebell[0m for a long
  moment. Her bell had gone green.
  Somebody had written WE'LL MISS YOU in
  the grime on her saloon window with a
  finger.

  "No," he said. "We're taking the long
  way."

  Teo frowned. "There isn't a long way."


